WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.710
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY: Thanks
for coming to the session

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.010
on building for Enterprise IoT.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.180
My name is Indranil Chakraborty.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:15.100
I'm one of the product guy at
Google Cloud Platform focusing

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.710
on IoT.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.550
And in this session what
we're going to cover

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:22.390
is we're going to cover
some of the key products

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:28.060
and services which Google has
for building IoT solution, some

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.680
of the key challenges
that we've seen

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.750
and we've heard from
developers and partners,

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.690
and how Google thinks about
addressing those challenges.

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:38.110
And at the end we'll
have a short demo.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:39.430
So let's get started.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:44.340
There you go.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.920
So imagine never being
stuck looking for parking.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.460
Imagine if every parking
lot, every parking space,

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.220
has a sensor embedded
in the space which

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.390
can data mine whether that
space is occupied or not.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.470
And it sends its
data to Cloud, so

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.900
that as you are driving,
looking for parking

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.570
in a city like Amsterdam,
or any other city,

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.010
you know exactly
where the parking

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.630
lot, what space is open, and
your car goes and parks there.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:18.240
Now imagine if in
the US it is believed

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.360
that there are around 800
million parking spaces.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.180
Imagine if all those spaces
have sensors embedded.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.240
Imagine the amount of data
that we generated in order

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.907
for us to figure out which
of the spaces is available,

00:01:29.907 --> 00:01:32.490
and send the data down to you,
the applications such as Google

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:33.690
Maps.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.110
Let's take some more examples.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.550
At Google we've used deep
learning-- machine learning--

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:45.750
to reduce the amount of energy
needed for cooling data centers

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.880
by almost 40%.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.350
So imagine if you can optimize
the power consumption in all

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.130
the commercial buildings by
applying the same principle.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.800
So where you collect
real-time data

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.860
from your edge [INAUDIBLE],,
from your coolers,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.340
from ambient temperatures,
humidity, lights, coolers,

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.000
and all the data, and you
build machine-learning models

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.550
using historical data
over time, and then you

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:15.010
apply those predictions
based on these models

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.470
to optimize for your
cloud consumption

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.660
in commercial building.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.650
So such applications like this
could be really revolutionary.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.380
And we seeing some
patterns in the industry

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.510
on some of the customers
building such applications

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.340
as well.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.950
Utilities is another example.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:36.360
Utility providers have been
installing smart meters

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.550
over the last couple of years.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:43.230
And now, by connecting
the smart meters to Cloud,

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:48.060
they can get a real-time view
of power consumption in a city,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.000
and also break it down by
neighborhoods within a city

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.250
as well.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:58.980
Now they can compare the
real-time demand with forecast,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.070
and they can balance the power
distribution real time as well.

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.280
So these are some of the
revolutionary applications

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:11.880
we see when you use the sensor
data and our cloud platform

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.355
to really build a compelling
enterprise application.

00:03:14.355 --> 00:03:16.710
And very soon cities
will have millions

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:21.780
of smart meters sending energy
data, energy reading real time.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.170
So we believe that IoT
is at an inflection point

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.030
where we expect
significant growth

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.980
over the next couple of years.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.970
In fact, if you look
at the projection,

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:36.780
while mobile phones growth
has been much bigger

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:40.530
than personal computers,
a much higher growth wave

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.430
is expected for IoT.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:47.070
Depending on the
analyst report you read,

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:51.660
it is estimated that around 20
billion to 30 billion devices

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.930
will be connected
to internet by 2020.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.730
While each of
these devices might

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:01.260
send a few hundreds of
bytes every few seconds,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.110
or for minutes, or
an hour, cumulative

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.300
that's a massive
amount of data which

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.370
will be generated by
these billions of devices

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.590
connected to internet.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:14.580
So this creates a
couple of challenges.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.110
One is as you think about
building IoT solution,

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.450
one of the key
challenge is security.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.450
How do you make sure that
all these devices that

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:28.950
are connected are secured,
they are not compromised;

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:32.700
and also the data which is
being generated by these devices

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:36.600
are securely stored
on cloud or on prem?

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.340
Second is scale.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.500
So when you have
millions or even

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.890
billions of devices
connected to cloud,

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.760
how do you ensure that we can
build the application that

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.820
can ingest this
massive influx of data,

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.454
and store it cost effectively?

00:04:53.454 --> 00:04:54.870
And how do you
make sure that when

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.640
you have these
millions of devices,

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.910
that all these devices have
the right firmware version;

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.700
they have the right security
patches as well, so you're not

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:07.780
worried about fragmentation in
your fleet of deployed devices?

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.622
And then finally,
when you have data,

00:05:09.622 --> 00:05:10.830
what do you do with the data?

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:12.371
What do you do with
the mass of data?

00:05:12.371 --> 00:05:14.070
How do you get
actionable insights

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:17.910
so that you can really use
it to optimize your business

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:21.320
processes, or to glean
specific insights which

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.020
will help in your business?

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:26.970
So we've been working hard
over the last couple of years

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.670
to really make it easier for
developers to build the IoT

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:35.550
solution on Google Cloud
that is secure, scalable, as

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.620
well as you can get
actionable insight as well.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:44.280
I think you all know that Google
has around seven products--

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:49.020
consumer products-- that there
are more than 1 billion users--

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:53.100
active users-- that are
engaging with these products.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:57.030
So as part of our mission
to organize the world's

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:01.470
information, we've been
working with and dealing

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:07.080
with massive scale of data, big
data, for over a decade now.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:12.430
And in the process of
handling this scale of data,

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:15.810
we've also built one of
the world's best computer

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:19.800
infrastructure across
hardware, software, networking,

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.630
and operations.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.230
We built an infrastructure
such that for developers it's

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:29.400
just one computer, and it's
not a collection of computers.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.390
And developers, you just write
code, and write applications,

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:38.280
and we take care of running them
across different data centers,

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.850
or scaling them across different
infrastructures as well.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:44.340
In addition to our data
center infrastructure,

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.330
we've also been working hard in
building up our data management

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:51.300
and technical stack as well.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.440
So you've heard about
Pub/Sub, BigQuery, Bigtable.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.800
Everything that Google has
built which we use internally

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.920
for our own development, and
are used by internal developers,

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.200
are in the process of
being externalized.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:08.060
So recently you've heard about
TensorFlow, which we have also

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:10.290
packaged as Cloud ML.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:12.390
Spanner is another example.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:19.320
So we believe that we have the
industry-leading services when

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.150
it comes to data analytics
and machine learning.

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:24.870
And many of our developers,
partners, and enterprise

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.900
customers have been leveraging
these cloud platform

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:31.210
services to build
enterprise applications.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:37.950
But when it comes to IoT, the
same developers and partners

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.070
have asked before
that it's great

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.750
that you have the
services, but how

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:45.930
do we connect our
devices to Google Cloud,

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:48.560
and how do we manage those
devices to Google Cloud

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.980
so that we can start to benefit
from the machine learning

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:55.860
and data analytics
services that you have?

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.790
So last month, we're
pretty excited that we

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.820
announced a product
called Cloud IoT Core.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.940
It's a new service on
Google Cloud Platform,

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:09.030
with the singular
goal of making it

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:12.480
really easy for developers
to build enterprise IoT

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:14.140
applications.

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:17.380
What is Cloud IoT Core?

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:21.960
It's a fully managed service
to securely connect and manage

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:25.770
your devices on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.530
So what we've done
is first we have

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:33.799
added support for the industry
standard protocol MQQT.

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:36.320
As we talked to
partners and developers,

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:39.390
we realized that many
of these devices,

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:41.370
existing devices which
are already applied,

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:45.000
or even new devices,
they use MQQT

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.770
for machine-to-machine and even
machine-to-Cloud messaging.

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:53.760
So with Cloud IoT
Core, you can securely

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.910
connect your device
using TLS, and then

00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:00.510
use MQQT messaging protocol
for sending telemetry

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:01.330
to Google Cloud.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:07.210
The second key thing
of Cloud IoT Core,

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:09.880
is we scale internally.

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:12.700
Whether you're connecting
one device or you scale up

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:14.560
to millions of
device, you don't have

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:18.100
to worry about provisioning a
new node, or a new instance,

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:19.820
or a new infrastructure.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:21.220
We scale internally.

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:24.100
And we can handle millions
or even billions of devices

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:26.960
as you scale up or scale down.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.550
And we also tightly
integrated this

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:33.850
with Pub/Sub, which
allows you to get

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:37.462
a central view of your
globally disposed devices.

00:09:37.462 --> 00:09:38.920
I'm going to talk
a little bit more

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:41.980
about how that works
in a few minutes.

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:44.800
We've also added functionality--
some key functionalities--

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:46.420
for device management.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:51.370
So you can individually
configure your device

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:58.020
to make sure that each device
is authenticated separately.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:01.650
So we have support
for per device,

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:04.640
asymmetric keys-based
authentication using JSON Web

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:05.500
Token.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:07.420
And this ensures
that this in fact

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:09.040
limits the surface
area of attack.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:13.310
So even if a public key or a
private key is compromised,

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:15.820
it will only affect that
particular device, not

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:17.230
the rest of your fleet.

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:20.140
So we've added
per-device authentication

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:24.100
as part of a device management
capability with Cloud IoT Core.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:26.110
With Cloud IoT Core
you can also send

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:30.050
a configuration and updates
to your devices from the cloud

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:30.550
as well.

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:34.540
You can control your
devices from Google Cloud.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:40.420
And you can even control access
to users to a set of devices,

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:43.240
or a group or devices,
using Cloud IAM.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:46.390
Access can be granted at a
project level on Google Cloud,

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:47.890
or at a registry level.

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:51.610
If you have a signed in
Cloud IAM at a project level,

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:53.140
then all the
registries by default

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:55.240
will inherit those roles.

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:58.240
But you can refine it at
individual or registry level

00:10:58.240 --> 00:10:59.690
as well.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:03.100
And finally, we have
a rich set of APIs

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:06.250
for you to build your compelling
enterprise application

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:08.980
on top of Cloud IoT Core.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:14.350
So we've announced this last
month as a private beta.

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:17.110
And we're getting interesting,
good feedback from our partners

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:18.010
and developers.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:21.770
And we plan to make it available
more broadly in the next couple

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:22.270
of months.

00:11:24.807 --> 00:11:26.640
So I want to spend just
a little bit of time

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:30.360
on the resource model
for device management

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.120
capability on Cloud IoT Core.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:35.940
So let's say if you are
a device manufacturer,

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:39.750
and you have 10 different
categories of devices,

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:44.130
you will go to Google Cloud,
and you have a project.

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:46.950
Under project you can create
10 different registries,

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:51.470
each for your each of
your device category.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:55.040
And registry essentially
is a collection of devices.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:58.920
So you can create for each
category one registry,

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:01.920
and under each registry you
can register as many devices

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:02.500
as you want.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:06.570
You can have millions of
devices under each registry.

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:08.990
And a device, when
you register a device,

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:10.740
is essentially a
logical representation

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:12.340
of your physical device.

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:15.640
So we store the
credential for the device.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:17.980
So when you add a device,
or you register a device

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:22.710
to your registry, you have to
add the public key, which will

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:25.560
be used for authentication.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:28.410
We also store the configuration.

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:30.850
And eventually we'll
add states as well.

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:35.250
So it's an exact representation
of your physical device

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:36.780
on the cloud side.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:40.050
So you can have multiple
registries per project.

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:43.170
And each registry, you can
register as many devices

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:45.460
as you want.

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:48.450
So this really gives
you the ability

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:52.770
to manage a massive
scale of devices,

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:57.780
and you can group them based on
your, say, specific use case.

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.600
You can assign,
like as I mentioned,

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:03.310
a certain access control
at a registry level.

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:09.610
So for example, you can
create a Cloud IAM provision,

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:12.330
Cloud IAM role called
provisioner at a registry.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:14.910
So all the users which
belong to that role

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:21.180
will only be able to add or edit
device, but not remove or add

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:22.170
new registry.

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:24.930
So you have this granular
level of access control

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:27.210
that you can provide
at registry level.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:33.430
And as we were
building this product,

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:35.200
and releasing this
product, we realized,

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:39.040
and we understand that both
developers and enterprise

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:42.850
partners would want to first
build some quick prototype

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:47.210
to wet their feet before they
do full-scale development.

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:53.080
So we have a simple UI as well,
as part of Cloud IoT Core,

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:55.820
for management and
monitoring of devices.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:14:00.730
So what we see here is if you
go to the Core Cloud console,

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:03.670
on the dropdown menu there
will be a section for IoT Core.

00:14:03.670 --> 00:14:05.610
If you select,
click on IoT Core,

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:08.200
you would see all the
registries that has been already

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:09.910
added by you previously.

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:11.740
You can also create
a new registry,

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:16.250
and I'll show you the demo
later down in my session.

00:14:16.250 --> 00:14:18.695
So the top one shows a
list of all the registries

00:14:18.695 --> 00:14:19.570
which has been added.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:25.090
You can assign the ID to
it you can select a region

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:28.570
where you want all the
devices, register that device,

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:30.700
to have it stored as metadata.

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:32.710
And it shows the
different protocols

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:35.650
that registry devices support.

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:38.320
One interesting thing about
my IoT Core and registry

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:45.550
is you can assign a Pub/Sub
topic to a registry.

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:48.850
What that means
is all the device

00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:53.420
data which belongs to that
registry would be published

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:55.120
to that Pub/Sub topic.

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:57.190
So while each
individual device will

00:14:57.190 --> 00:14:59.650
be sent in publishing as
data on a particular topic,

00:14:59.650 --> 00:15:03.830
but it will get aggregated and
published at a Pub/Sub topic,

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:06.640
so you can get a real global
view of all the devices

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:09.340
by consuming data from
Pub/Sub topic as well.

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:12.700
And when you select
on the registry on UI,

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:16.000
you can get a list
of devices that are

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.010
registered in that registry.

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:21.670
And you can see some you have
simple status, such as there's

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:25.540
enable, disable, what was
the last message received

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:26.990
by that particular device.

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:29.300
So you can do some simple
monitoring as well.

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:31.074
And if you go drill
down even more,

00:15:31.074 --> 00:15:32.740
you can select on the
particular device,

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:34.240
and get some
additional information

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:37.220
about that particular
device as well.

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:39.460
So it's a simple way
for you to get started

00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:43.552
where you can create some
registry, and add some devices,

00:15:43.552 --> 00:15:45.010
and start playing
with the product,

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.680
and really start connecting
the data to build

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:51.300
a simple application.

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:52.980
As we launch IoT
Core, we have also

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:57.810
announced partnership agreement
with some of our leading device

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:00.270
manufacturers and
software vendors

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:05.310
as well, such as Intel,
Microchip, NXP, Sierra

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:07.990
Wireless, Arm.

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:10.650
All these hardware
partners have agreed

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:12.750
to support Cloud IoT Core.

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:15.750
And we're working very
closely with these partners

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:18.930
to make it really
easy for developers

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:21.510
to have these partner
devices seamlessly

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:22.950
connect to Cloud IoT Core.

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:26.850
So imagine, the way to work
is if you purchase a device

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:32.060
from NXP, it will have a
very simple instruction

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:34.677
to show you how to connect
that device to Cloud IoT Core.

00:16:34.677 --> 00:16:36.510
In fact, moving forward,
we are working hard

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:39.579
with these partners so
that when you buy a device,

00:16:39.579 --> 00:16:41.370
and it boots up, it
will just automatically

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:44.160
connect to Cloud IoT Core.

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:48.150
And in fact, thanks to some of
our partners, such as Mongoose,

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:53.460
you can connect any
microcontroller.

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:57.530
This is a tiny MCU device
built by Expressif.

00:16:57.530 --> 00:17:00.900
It's an ESP8266.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:02.880
You can have this
tiny device directly

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:05.040
connect to Cloud
IoT Core as well.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:08.130
So you can set up
a TLS connection

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:11.400
using the software
made by Mongoose OS,

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:15.000
and their sample
code, which shows you

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:16.980
how you can connect
these devices directly

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:18.510
to Cloud IoT Core.

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:22.484
They have the ability
to generate public key,

00:17:22.484 --> 00:17:24.359
so you can authenticate
each of these devices

00:17:24.359 --> 00:17:25.980
individually, and
in a secure way.

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:28.530
So we're pretty
excited about this.

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:32.340
And so we'll continue
to work hard with a lot

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:33.810
of our new partners as well.

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:36.990
So you will hear a lot more
about our partner ecosystem

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:38.170
going forward.

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:43.230
And I would really encourage
you guys to visit our site

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:46.730
cloud.google.com/iot/partners.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:51.150
So see the list of devices,
software partners with whom we

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:53.010
have agreement for
Cloud IoT Core.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:54.760
And we're working with
a lot more of them,

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:59.600
so you'll hear a lot
more down the road.

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:02.810
So I talked about
securely connecting

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:05.240
your device, and some
aspects of how you can

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:07.490
manage those devices on cloud.

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:09.890
But once you have
connected the device,

00:18:09.890 --> 00:18:13.070
how do you ingest the
massive amount of data

00:18:13.070 --> 00:18:15.590
from your millions or
billions of devices?

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:18.850
And Pub/Sub is great for that.

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:22.780
What Pub/Sub allows you to
do is a couple of key things.

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:24.200
One is a global service.

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:27.950
So you may have devices
dispersed across the globe.

00:18:27.950 --> 00:18:31.640
But with Pub/Sub, you can get
a central view of all the data

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:35.300
that has been published
across all these devices.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:39.200
What Pub/Sub also has is it
has durable persistent message.

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:44.390
So it promises that the
message from a device

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:49.865
is stored until acknowledgement,
or a return message

00:18:49.865 --> 00:18:51.860
is sent back to the device.

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:53.300
So this makes a
lot of sense when

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:56.300
you have a use case where you
don't want to-- particularly

00:18:56.300 --> 00:18:59.030
for medical devices-- where you
don't want to lose any data.

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:03.150
Pub/Sub is a great way to sort
of route the data downwards.

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:09.800
And Pub/Sub also helps us to
decouple your upstream devices

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:12.350
from a downstream application.

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:14.060
Because with Pub/Sub,
you can continue

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:16.850
to iterate on your
application which

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:20.660
is consuming the data without
needing to change or update

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:22.910
your upstream devices.

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:27.470
And you can, for example,
have Cloud Function consume,

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:29.840
subscribe to a
topic from Pub/Sub,

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:32.000
which you can use to
build some simple rules.

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:36.200
You can use Cloud Dataflow for
some complex event processing.

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:38.990
Or even you can use an
existing ETL pipeline

00:19:38.990 --> 00:19:41.360
to consume the data
which has been generated

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:43.940
by all these devices
connected to Cloud IoT Core

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:46.130
through Pub/Sub.

00:19:46.130 --> 00:19:49.700
So with Cloud IoT Core and
Pub/Sub, and the conjunction

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:51.800
of this, you can securely
connect your devices,

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:55.130
but also ingest
massive amount of data

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:57.020
without worrying
about provisioning

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:00.940
or creating new infrastructure.

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:02.880
So once you've
connected and ingested,

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:05.440
next step is what do
you do with the data?

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:08.280
So here I've listed
some key examples

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:10.920
of the different services,
existing cloud services, which

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:14.430
you can use for
storage and analysis.

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:20.110
So BigQuery is great
for calibrating,

00:20:20.110 --> 00:20:22.330
and the understanding
of the data.

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:25.470
So you can have hundreds of
billions of rows of data,

00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:28.640
and you can do some quick
query to really understand

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:31.050
what are the other
typical trends you're

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:32.700
seeing on the data.

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:36.270
You can then use Dataflow
to clean up the data

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:37.999
and build some simple models.

00:20:37.999 --> 00:20:39.540
You can create some
simple regression

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:41.220
models using Dataflow.

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:44.310
And then further down, you can
then use machine learning--

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:48.000
Cloud ML-- to create, to
train, to intensive flow model,

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:50.130
using the data which you
have generated and stored

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:52.920
on BigQuery, or even Bigtable.

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:56.070
So we have all these services--

00:20:56.070 --> 00:20:59.850
to name a few, BigQuery,
Dataflow, Big Data

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:01.500
Lab, and the Cloud ML--

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:03.990
for once you've
gathered the data,

00:21:03.990 --> 00:21:06.550
to glean insights and
build models, train

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:11.050
models on those data as well.

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:17.790
So here's a view of how
we see the entire IoT

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:20.670
platform as you think about
building IoT solution on Google

00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:21.630
Cloud.

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.910
So you have Cloud
IoT Core, which

00:21:23.910 --> 00:21:26.820
sits between the devices
and Google Cloud,

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:29.940
for you to securely connect
and manage your data.

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.140
The data gets
published to Pub/Sub.

00:21:32.140 --> 00:21:34.980
And from there you can
write some simple rules

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:36.210
using Cloud Function.

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:37.680
For example, you
can say every time

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:39.900
the median temperature,
or the temperature

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.510
goes above 90 degrees, I
want to turn on the fan.

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:45.270
So you can write some simple
rules using Cloud Function,

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:48.180
and you can send command
back to your devices

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:49.590
using Cloud IoT Core.

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:52.860
Or you can use Dataflow for
doing some complex event

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:54.400
processing as well.

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:57.150
And once you have
cleaned up the data

00:21:57.150 --> 00:21:59.490
based on using
Dataflow jobs, you

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:01.440
can store it in
BigQuery or Bigtable

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:06.150
for further analysis
and report generation.

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:09.210
Then you can use Cloud
ML, as I mentioned,

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:12.210
to build some machine
learning models, train models.

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:13.800
And then use prediction--

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:17.250
Cloud ML predictions--
to act on the data

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:22.000
and send some relevant commands
back to the devices as well.

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:25.530
So overall, on the
Cloud side I talked

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:31.400
about how you can connect to
your devices to Google Cloud

00:22:31.400 --> 00:22:34.470
ingest them at scale
without worrying about

00:22:34.470 --> 00:22:37.050
whether it's a few devices
or millions of devices

00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:39.230
without needing to provision
any infrastructure.

00:22:39.230 --> 00:22:40.750
And you can use an
existing service,

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:43.470
such as Cloud ML, and
BigQuery, and Datalab

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:45.990
for data analysis and storage.

00:22:45.990 --> 00:22:50.070
I want to spend
some time on how we

00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:54.450
can take care of security,
scale and insights at the edge

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:55.680
itself.

00:22:55.680 --> 00:23:00.300
And we have Android
Things, which

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:04.580
is a fully managed operating
system for devices and edge

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:06.400
gateways.

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:10.950
So Android Things essentially
is Android operating system

00:23:10.950 --> 00:23:15.420
that is optimized to
run on IoT devices.

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:16.620
Now what does that mean?

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:19.805
So what we have done is
we have taken the Android,

00:23:19.805 --> 00:23:23.700
the stock Android, and we
have removed the components

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:26.010
which is not relevant to IoT.

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:30.840
For example we have removed all
the notification, the system

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:36.300
uIPs, user applications; and
then added some specific IoT

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:40.930
components, such as Peripheral
I/O and device driver support.

00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:44.640
So you can connect additional
component to your devices,

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:48.300
and then run Android
Things on them as well.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:51.360
And some of the key things which
you get with Android Things

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:55.290
is number one, if you
want to build scale--

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:58.530
so if you want to build
some quick prototype for all

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:02.268
the Android
developers out there--

00:24:02.268 --> 00:24:05.730
you can use a familiar
Android ecosystem,

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:09.290
such as the Android
SDK, the Android Studio,

00:24:09.290 --> 00:24:12.720
to build those applications,
compile it into APK,

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:14.040
and run it on Android Things.

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:18.780
You can even use
some existing APK

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:21.180
that you have built for
Android mobile phone

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:23.265
and port it into
Android things as well.

00:24:23.265 --> 00:24:28.620
So this really makes
it, for the developer,

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:32.100
to really have built quick
prototypes on the device

00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:36.400
by using the familiar
Android ecosystem.

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:39.870
And we already have more than
$2 billion monthly active phones

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:41.970
and tablets which
are running Android.

00:24:41.970 --> 00:24:45.190
And now we're extending
it to IoT as well.

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:50.100
Second is with Android Things
we have used Android Things

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:52.316
for security on
the device as well.

00:24:52.316 --> 00:24:53.940
So the great thing
about Android Things

00:24:53.940 --> 00:24:56.430
is since it leverages
the key component

00:24:56.430 --> 00:24:58.320
of security for Android--

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:01.550
so it has a secure boot.

00:25:01.550 --> 00:25:03.890
The applications are signed.

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:09.680
And Google manages the former
version of Android Things.

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:12.230
So we will make sure that
all the Android Things

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:16.700
devices has the latest firmware,
has the right security patches,

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:19.160
so that none of them
can be compromised.

00:25:19.160 --> 00:25:22.790
Of course, in cases where
the enterprise customer

00:25:22.790 --> 00:25:24.950
or developers want
more control in terms

00:25:24.950 --> 00:25:27.440
of when that patch
should be deployed,

00:25:27.440 --> 00:25:30.856
that is available as well.

00:25:30.856 --> 00:25:33.230
And Android Things, I'll talk
more about designer things,

00:25:33.230 --> 00:25:35.917
also work seamlessly
with Cloud IoT Core.

00:25:35.917 --> 00:25:38.000
And we are working harder
to make this integration

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:40.550
much more simpler.

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:44.080
So that was on the software
side of Android Things.

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:47.240
On the hardware layer,
we're working hard

00:25:47.240 --> 00:25:50.780
to remove the complexity
of building a device.

00:25:50.780 --> 00:25:54.560
So we're working
with silicon partners

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:59.450
to certify their SOM,
essentially a system on module.

00:25:59.450 --> 00:26:02.780
So SOM will come with
your CPU, memory, storage,

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:04.160
and connectivity.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:08.840
And all the components
to run our Android things

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:09.960
are already built in.

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:14.470
So you can start using
the certified devices

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:17.930
to add it to your base board,
and add necessary components

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:19.190
as you feel.

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:22.250
And since the SOM is
already certified,

00:26:22.250 --> 00:26:24.560
you don't have to go through
the certification process

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:26.260
separately.

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:30.080
For enterprise
customers, we already

00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:33.770
have partners such as
NXP, Intel, Qualcomm,

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:37.490
who are already building
certified SOM which

00:26:37.490 --> 00:26:38.340
runs Android Things.

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:39.950
So you can just take
those components

00:26:39.950 --> 00:26:44.090
and start testing Android Things
for your specific use case.

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:50.270
And finally, the last
layer for Android things

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:54.002
is really the services.

00:26:54.002 --> 00:26:59.470
So we want to make sure
that with Android Things

00:26:59.470 --> 00:27:01.390
you can leverage
the existing Google

00:27:01.390 --> 00:27:06.880
services as well, such as
location services, assistance

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:09.970
service, Chromecast
cast service, as well

00:27:09.970 --> 00:27:13.120
as our new services,
such as TensorFlow.

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:16.090
So great thing about
Android is number one,

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:19.270
it seamlessly work
with Cloud IoT.

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:22.801
So if you have Android Things
device, it is very easy for you

00:27:22.801 --> 00:27:24.300
to connect those
device to Cloud IoT

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:27.410
and start leveraging the
cloud platform for it.

00:27:27.410 --> 00:27:33.010
And secondly, Android
Things can use TensorFlow.

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:34.960
You can use TensorFlow
on Android Things

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:40.070
to run machine learning
model locally on the device.

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:45.490
So this works in the use
case where latency matters,

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:48.820
or you don't want to send
every information to the cloud.

00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:52.520
You can run your TensorFlow
model locally at the edge,

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:54.580
and get the same
level of insights

00:27:54.580 --> 00:27:56.500
as you would do with Cloud.

00:27:56.500 --> 00:28:00.350
And I'll talk more, give
some specific example.

00:28:00.350 --> 00:28:03.470
So let's take a
specific example.

00:28:03.470 --> 00:28:07.600
Let's say you want
to check the quality

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:09.380
control of your product.

00:28:09.380 --> 00:28:11.710
You're making a
pizza, and you want

00:28:11.710 --> 00:28:13.540
to make sure that
every pizza that's

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:19.150
been made in an assembly line
is meeting your quality bar.

00:28:19.150 --> 00:28:22.540
There's no template, so you
can use machine learning

00:28:22.540 --> 00:28:25.360
to look at the shape of
the pizza that comes up,

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:29.260
the color, some of the texture,
the temperature, and all

00:28:29.260 --> 00:28:31.540
of that, and really
figure out which one

00:28:31.540 --> 00:28:33.514
is meeting your quality bar.

00:28:33.514 --> 00:28:34.930
So in this case,
what you would do

00:28:34.930 --> 00:28:39.880
is you'll have a camera
which is capturing

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:43.180
the pictures of the pizza
as it's being manufactured.

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:47.980
And you will have
trained a model on Cloud

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:51.190
which automatically can
detect whether a pizza is

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:53.200
meeting your bar or not.

00:28:53.200 --> 00:28:55.720
And then you can push
that TensorFlow model

00:28:55.720 --> 00:28:59.590
into the camera which is
running Android Things.

00:28:59.590 --> 00:29:03.580
So as the camera is
capturing the picture of each

00:29:03.580 --> 00:29:08.020
of those pizza, it is locally
running the trained model

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:11.230
to see if it is really
meets your quality bar,

00:29:11.230 --> 00:29:14.350
or should it be remade,
or should it be rejected.

00:29:14.350 --> 00:29:18.080
And then over time, you
can send part of the feed

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:22.300
to Cloud through Cloud IoT
Core so that you can constantly

00:29:22.300 --> 00:29:24.520
retrain and update
that model, and then

00:29:24.520 --> 00:29:28.800
download the model back
into the local device

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:30.650
for local processing.

00:29:30.650 --> 00:29:33.940
So this kind of example
with Android Things

00:29:33.940 --> 00:29:35.770
and with Cloud IoT,
as you can see,

00:29:35.770 --> 00:29:41.120
you not only get the benefit
of the massive infrastructure

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:43.930
on Cloud for training purposes,
for training your model,

00:29:43.930 --> 00:29:46.600
but you can also,
where it makes sense,

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:49.600
you can run some of the
processing locally as well.

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:55.900
And with Android Things, it
can build APK applications

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:58.750
where you can do some
filtration, or some edge

00:29:58.750 --> 00:30:02.380
processing on the device,
and send the rest of the data

00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:04.340
to the cloud for
further analysis.

00:30:08.696 --> 00:30:14.330
So when you think about IoT,
Android Things connecting

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:18.480
to IoT, there are a couple of
steps which needs to happen.

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:24.670
So in this case, when we'll
create a cloud project,

00:30:24.670 --> 00:30:28.890
you'd have to enable
the Cloud IoT API.

00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:35.530
And Android Things
has a key store,

00:30:35.530 --> 00:30:39.010
where the private key
is protected and stored,

00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:40.410
so you cannot tamper with it.

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:43.780
But you can extract the public
key from the Android Things

00:30:43.780 --> 00:30:44.770
key store.

00:30:44.770 --> 00:30:47.200
And then you take
that public key,

00:30:47.200 --> 00:30:49.480
and you register that
device with that public key,

00:30:49.480 --> 00:30:51.580
with Cloud IoT Core.

00:30:51.580 --> 00:30:56.890
And you are, for registration
you can use CLIs or command

00:30:56.890 --> 00:31:01.780
line tools which we have, or
even you can use some of the UI

00:31:01.780 --> 00:31:03.230
as well.

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:05.680
And once you've
registered the device,

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:07.750
then it's really the
third step is really

00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:12.325
to configure your APK to send
MQQT messages to Cloud IoT

00:31:12.325 --> 00:31:12.940
Core.

00:31:12.940 --> 00:31:17.110
And we have a sample, which
is already posted on GitHub.

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:20.510
You can look it up on
gitup.com/androidthings.

00:31:20.510 --> 00:31:23.550
I really encourage you
guys to search for it.

00:31:23.550 --> 00:31:26.050
And there's a simple
application sample code

00:31:26.050 --> 00:31:30.509
which you can use to build
an APK for [INAUDIBLE] device

00:31:30.509 --> 00:31:32.050
which will connect
to Cloud IoT Core,

00:31:32.050 --> 00:31:37.060
and start sending database
from your device to Cloud.

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:40.330
And you can even look at some
of the TensorFlow pieces well.

00:31:40.330 --> 00:31:44.090
So with Android
Things on the edge,

00:31:44.090 --> 00:31:46.830
and with Cloud IoT
Core on the cloud

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:49.120
side for secure connection
and device management,

00:31:49.120 --> 00:31:52.210
you can really start
thinking about building

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:55.660
really compelling application
for enterprise-specific use

00:31:55.660 --> 00:31:58.450
cases.

00:31:58.450 --> 00:32:01.810
So let me spend some
time on some quick demos.

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:06.000
So if we switch to the computer.

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:07.650
Great.

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:12.720
So this is our cloud, Google
Cloud platform console.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:18.370
And if you go to
the menu bar, there

00:32:18.370 --> 00:32:20.610
will be a section
called IoT Core.

00:32:20.610 --> 00:32:22.560
If you select on
that, what happens

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:28.860
is you would see a list of all
the registries that has already

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:31.080
been added and created before.

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:33.540
So if you want to
start a new registry,

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:35.850
you go to Cloud
IoT Cloud Console.

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:37.550
You just click that.

00:32:37.550 --> 00:32:42.200
Let me expand it
so you can see it.

00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:45.160
Let's say I create a
new registry-- say,

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:47.200
Amsterdam Session.

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:52.180
You can select a
region where you

00:32:52.180 --> 00:32:55.660
want the registry and
all the device metadata

00:32:55.660 --> 00:32:58.380
for the registry to restore.

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:00.610
Here we select a
pops-up topic where

00:33:00.610 --> 00:33:02.860
all the data from all
the devices registered

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:05.170
to this registry will
be eventually published.

00:33:05.170 --> 00:33:07.740
So for now I'll just select one.

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:10.090
And then you create.

00:33:10.090 --> 00:33:14.520
Once you do that, you have
created a new registry

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:15.970
called Amsterdam Session.

00:33:15.970 --> 00:33:20.350
And then after that, you
can add a device as well.

00:33:20.350 --> 00:33:23.020
So every physical
device which you

00:33:23.020 --> 00:33:26.270
want to connect to
Cloud IoT Core you

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:29.260
have to register the
device into a registry.

00:33:29.260 --> 00:33:30.940
And then once the
device connects,

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:33.023
we will use that information
which you have passed

00:33:33.023 --> 00:33:34.340
to authenticate the device.

00:33:34.340 --> 00:33:37.840
So when you add a device, you
can give any device ID name

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:38.800
here.

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:42.280
By default, it will be enabled,
unless you want to disable it.

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:44.650
And as I've mentioned,
for authentication

00:33:44.650 --> 00:33:48.670
we use per-device
private public key pair.

00:33:48.670 --> 00:33:51.370
And we support two
crypto algorithms.

00:33:51.370 --> 00:33:54.520
So we support RSA
as well as ECC.

00:33:54.520 --> 00:33:57.910
So you can choose which crypto
algorithm you want to use.

00:33:57.910 --> 00:34:00.760
And assuming you have
the private key, either

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:03.200
you can generate separately,
or you have it stored.

00:34:03.200 --> 00:34:07.060
You can extend the public key,
and add it here, and then just

00:34:07.060 --> 00:34:10.699
add, add and
register the device.

00:34:10.699 --> 00:34:15.320
You can register up to three
public keys per device.

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:16.719
You can also specify an expiry.

00:34:16.719 --> 00:34:20.389
By default we won't
expire the public key.

00:34:20.389 --> 00:34:22.810
But if you'd put
an expiry, then we

00:34:22.810 --> 00:34:28.020
will not use a public
key for authentication.

00:34:28.020 --> 00:34:29.670
So let me show you.

00:34:32.610 --> 00:34:38.420
Since I've added a session, if
I go to a new registry which

00:34:38.420 --> 00:34:41.420
I have created before called
Group Register, what I see

00:34:41.420 --> 00:34:51.489
is there are all these devices,
which I've registered before.

00:34:51.489 --> 00:34:53.770
And they're all
in disabled state.

00:34:53.770 --> 00:34:57.090
So what I've done is you
think about the use case where

00:34:57.090 --> 00:35:00.250
if you have a large
amount of devices which

00:35:00.250 --> 00:35:02.630
you want to connect to cloud.

00:35:02.630 --> 00:35:05.200
So when you register
the device, by default

00:35:05.200 --> 00:35:07.330
you would register
them as disabled.

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:10.600
And when you know that the
devices has been installed

00:35:10.600 --> 00:35:12.070
and has been
provisioned, then you

00:35:12.070 --> 00:35:13.900
can enable it on the
Cloud side so they

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:15.720
can start connecting
to Cloud IoT Core

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:17.300
and start sending data.

00:35:17.300 --> 00:35:20.342
So by default all of
them are disabled here.

00:35:20.342 --> 00:35:21.800
And the device ID
here, by the way,

00:35:21.800 --> 00:35:24.350
is just the MAC address
we have used here.

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:27.940
So as I mentioned, we
have a rich set of APIs

00:35:27.940 --> 00:35:33.040
which you can use for
registering your devices.

00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:35.650
You can use the UI, but
you can also have the APIs.

00:35:35.650 --> 00:35:37.570
So I have a mobile app here.

00:35:37.570 --> 00:35:41.410
And right now what it does is
it connects to Cloud IoT Core,

00:35:41.410 --> 00:35:43.480
the rest API on IoT Core.

00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:47.907
And what I'll do is I will say
I've looked at the devices.

00:35:47.907 --> 00:35:48.740
They're ready to go.

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:50.630
So I want to enable
all these devices

00:35:50.630 --> 00:35:53.530
so they can start connecting
and start sending data.

00:35:53.530 --> 00:35:56.080
So I have an app here
which connects to Cloud IoT

00:35:56.080 --> 00:35:57.600
Core using the rest API.

00:35:57.600 --> 00:35:59.530
And I'll just say
enable devices.

00:35:59.530 --> 00:36:03.640
And you would see that the
devices starts to get enabled.

00:36:03.640 --> 00:36:06.340
And as it's enabled, as the
device starts to connect,

00:36:06.340 --> 00:36:08.110
we can actually go
to each of the device

00:36:08.110 --> 00:36:11.110
and see when was the last
time we saw a message.

00:36:11.110 --> 00:36:16.900
So this is a simple way of
batch registering your devices.

00:36:16.900 --> 00:36:19.810
And then when you're ready
for the devices to connect,

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:22.370
you can enable these
devices as well.

00:36:22.370 --> 00:36:30.340
Now the other thing I want to
show you is what we have done

00:36:30.340 --> 00:36:37.270
is we have taken a large number
of Raspberry Pi devices, which

00:36:37.270 --> 00:36:39.880
has a humidity
sensor, a temperature

00:36:39.880 --> 00:36:43.900
sensor, a luminosity sensor,
and a pressure and motion sensor

00:36:43.900 --> 00:36:44.810
as well.

00:36:44.810 --> 00:36:47.470
And we have deployed it
across our Google Office

00:36:47.470 --> 00:36:49.690
in Mountain View,
in San Francisco,

00:36:49.690 --> 00:36:52.474
and in some of the
other offices as well.

00:36:52.474 --> 00:36:53.890
And what we're
doing with those is

00:36:53.890 --> 00:36:56.260
we are collecting data
on a regular basis.

00:36:56.260 --> 00:36:58.930
All these devices are
sending data per second,

00:36:58.930 --> 00:37:01.070
or sometimes a couple
of times a second.

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:02.500
And we have created a dashboard.

00:37:02.500 --> 00:37:06.010
So this registry,
weather station,

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:08.830
has a list of all the
devices which are registered.

00:37:08.830 --> 00:37:13.310
And if I select, let's
say, the top device here,

00:37:13.310 --> 00:37:21.100
it'll show you that the
device has been lost.

00:37:21.100 --> 00:37:24.460
It's recently
published data at 2:38.

00:37:24.460 --> 00:37:25.810
And there was an error.

00:37:25.810 --> 00:37:29.520
Sometimes when there are
errors, we can see that as well.

00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:31.010
You see the public key here.

00:37:31.010 --> 00:37:32.760
You can also add an
additional public key.

00:37:35.350 --> 00:37:37.330
So you can drill
down to each device

00:37:37.330 --> 00:37:39.840
and see the status, when
was the last message sent,

00:37:39.840 --> 00:37:43.032
so you know whether the device
is actually functioning or not.

00:37:43.032 --> 00:37:44.740
But if you want to
actually see the data,

00:37:44.740 --> 00:37:48.680
you can pull the data
from Pub/Sub topic.

00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:51.410
The other interesting
thing about the device

00:37:51.410 --> 00:37:58.990
is when you go to this section,
you can quickly sort of eyeball

00:37:58.990 --> 00:38:02.740
and see, all right, this device
has sent data through the DL,

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:05.920
but the second device hasn't
sent any data since May 16th.

00:38:05.920 --> 00:38:07.480
So something is off there.

00:38:07.480 --> 00:38:09.670
So you can sort of
drill down and look

00:38:09.670 --> 00:38:14.950
at the logs, or sort of start
investigating and debugging it

00:38:14.950 --> 00:38:17.090
as well.

00:38:17.090 --> 00:38:19.660
We also have on the
UI the ability for you

00:38:19.660 --> 00:38:22.190
to send a configuration
update as well.

00:38:22.190 --> 00:38:24.850
So let me take a
quick look here.

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:27.410
And we have a demo
we have created.

00:38:27.410 --> 00:38:30.410
There are a couple of devices
which are registered here.

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:33.550
So I can just go to [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:33.550 --> 00:38:38.050
This is a relay device which is
sitting in Mountain View now.

00:38:38.050 --> 00:38:43.390
But what I can do is I can
look at the history of all

00:38:43.390 --> 00:38:47.200
the configuration updates
which was sent from the cloud

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:48.670
to the device.

00:38:48.670 --> 00:38:55.300
So what it does is if you send
a message to it saying fan

00:38:55.300 --> 00:38:58.570
on colon true or false,
it'll turn on or off

00:38:58.570 --> 00:39:01.690
based on what message you
send it them, to the relay.

00:39:01.690 --> 00:39:05.980
So you can see all the messages
which were sent last month.

00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:06.800
You click on it.

00:39:06.800 --> 00:39:09.410
You can actually see what
was sent to the device.

00:39:09.410 --> 00:39:13.070
So it's a very
simple JSON object

00:39:13.070 --> 00:39:18.620
which says fan on equal to
false, or true in that way.

00:39:18.620 --> 00:39:20.830
And what you can
do here from UI is

00:39:20.830 --> 00:39:23.300
if you want to send a
message to the device,

00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:27.310
you can select, just say,
text, and you say fan on.

00:39:27.310 --> 00:39:31.900
I can change it
to true and send.

00:39:31.900 --> 00:39:33.020
It'll send it.

00:39:33.020 --> 00:39:36.560
And it'll automatically update
it here, listed here as well.

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:39.140
And when we get an
acknowledgment back

00:39:39.140 --> 00:39:42.380
from the device you would
see it in the device details.

00:39:48.481 --> 00:39:50.480
So this is still waiting
for an acknowledgement,

00:39:50.480 --> 00:39:53.750
because the device is in
[INAUDIBLE],, and it's not up.

00:39:53.750 --> 00:39:57.140
But you will get to see the
last time when the device sent

00:39:57.140 --> 00:40:00.380
an acknowledgment back based on
the configuration update which

00:40:00.380 --> 00:40:01.190
was sent.

00:40:01.190 --> 00:40:05.410
So it's a simple UI tool for
you to create a quick registry,

00:40:05.410 --> 00:40:09.680
build some quick prototypes,
send commands to the devices.

00:40:09.680 --> 00:40:11.660
You can also receive
data as well.

00:40:11.660 --> 00:40:15.800
So another thing
we have is this is

00:40:15.800 --> 00:40:18.500
a dashboard which we have
built using Cloud IoT Core.

00:40:18.500 --> 00:40:21.080
So we have all these devices
which are, as I mentioned,

00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:23.670
deployed across Google offices.

00:40:23.670 --> 00:40:26.960
And this is collecting
the data real time.

00:40:26.960 --> 00:40:32.460
So if I go to a specific device,
I can just look at it here.

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:35.410
And as you can see,
I'm getting messages

00:40:35.410 --> 00:40:37.950
pretty much every second, or
every two seconds in updates.

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:40.400
So it has the temperature,
humidity, light,

00:40:40.400 --> 00:40:43.760
and pressure data.

00:40:43.760 --> 00:40:46.920
And you can collect all
this data real time.

00:40:46.920 --> 00:40:49.310
You can create some
simple dashboard.

00:40:49.310 --> 00:40:51.050
And we are storing
it in BigQuery,

00:40:51.050 --> 00:40:54.080
so you can do some
subsequent analysis

00:40:54.080 --> 00:40:57.110
based on this data as well.

00:40:57.110 --> 00:41:03.530
So to just wrap it up, can
we go back to the slides?

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:09.630
Perfect.

00:41:09.630 --> 00:41:12.040
So we talked about
three key things.

00:41:12.040 --> 00:41:15.780
One is on the Cloud side,
with Cloud IoT Core,

00:41:15.780 --> 00:41:19.800
you can now securely connect
and manage your fleet of devices

00:41:19.800 --> 00:41:21.840
so that you can collect
data from your devices

00:41:21.840 --> 00:41:25.410
and start using our
data analytics ingestion

00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:28.450
and storage and machine learning
capabilities on this data.

00:41:28.450 --> 00:41:34.590
And we also have Android Things
for edge device, which you can

00:41:34.590 --> 00:41:37.050
get the same level of security.

00:41:37.050 --> 00:41:39.060
You can run some
local TensorFlow

00:41:39.060 --> 00:41:40.870
to get insights at the edge.

00:41:40.870 --> 00:41:44.970
And you can also manage
all these devices at scale,

00:41:44.970 --> 00:41:47.400
because Google takes
care of the former update

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:49.090
and former version as well.

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:54.720
Now the Android Things work
seamlessly with Cloud IoT Core.

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:56.767
But you can connect
to any device.

00:41:56.767 --> 00:41:58.350
It doesn't have to
run Android Things.

00:41:58.350 --> 00:42:02.850
You can connect any device
which is running either DBN,

00:42:02.850 --> 00:42:07.110
or some other operating
former version.

00:42:07.110 --> 00:42:09.180
As long as you can
connect, you can

00:42:09.180 --> 00:42:12.600
use MQQT to connect to Cloud IoT
Core and start sending message.

00:42:12.600 --> 00:42:16.440
So as the example I gave
you is this small MCU, which

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:20.160
is not running Android
Things, but it runs a small OS

00:42:20.160 --> 00:42:21.300
by Mongoose.

00:42:21.300 --> 00:42:23.060
It runs and supports
MQQT in TLS,

00:42:23.060 --> 00:42:24.810
and so it can connect
to Cloud IoT Core,

00:42:24.810 --> 00:42:26.684
and start sending the
messages there as well.

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:34.170
So a couple of
quick, next things,

00:42:34.170 --> 00:42:36.640
is we have some help links.

00:42:36.640 --> 00:42:38.550
So for Android
Things, you can go

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:42.690
to developer.android.com/things.

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:48.400
For IoT Core, it's
cloud.google.com/iot-core.

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:51.210
IoT Core is in
private beta, so you

00:42:51.210 --> 00:42:54.880
have to request access
to start using it.

00:42:54.880 --> 00:42:57.720
And we review our
access request,

00:42:57.720 --> 00:43:00.480
and we were looking at
it, and we grant access

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:02.460
based on your use case.

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:04.650
We will, over the
next couple of months,

00:43:04.650 --> 00:43:07.747
make it more broadly available
so you can start using it.

00:43:07.747 --> 00:43:09.330
But if you want to
use, I'll encourage

00:43:09.330 --> 00:43:11.830
you guys to go to the
site and request access

00:43:11.830 --> 00:43:13.920
so you can start
using it as well.

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:17.690
And there's a developer
community site as well.

00:43:17.690 --> 00:43:18.310
That's it.

00:43:18.310 --> 00:43:18.970
So thanks.

00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:23.310
And if you have questions, do
we have time for questions?

00:43:26.600 --> 00:43:28.051
We have some time?

00:43:28.051 --> 00:43:28.550
OK.

00:43:28.550 --> 00:43:31.230
I can take one or two
questions in two minutes.

00:43:31.230 --> 00:43:33.060
Otherwise I'll be
standing outside

00:43:33.060 --> 00:43:35.062
if you have more questions.

00:43:40.804 --> 00:43:44.530
AUDIENCE: Will you also
connect to older IoT platforms?

00:43:44.530 --> 00:43:46.530
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY: The
question is, would you

00:43:46.530 --> 00:43:48.880
connect to other IoT platforms?

00:43:48.880 --> 00:43:53.190
So if the use case is that you
have your devices connecting

00:43:53.190 --> 00:43:57.270
to Cloud IoT Core, because we
publish our data to Pub/Sub,

00:43:57.270 --> 00:44:00.420
you can consume that
data, and you can send it

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:02.040
to other IoT platform as well.

00:44:04.980 --> 00:44:09.180
So you can send it to your
own, in-built IoT solution,

00:44:09.180 --> 00:44:12.550
or you can send it to some
other public cloud provider.

00:44:12.550 --> 00:44:17.650
But if your question is around
if I have my own IoT solution,

00:44:17.650 --> 00:44:19.770
IoT platform, and how
do I send the data,

00:44:19.770 --> 00:44:22.880
you could still use it using
cloud-to-cloud adapter.

00:44:22.880 --> 00:44:24.510
So you can directly
connect to Pub/Sub,

00:44:24.510 --> 00:44:27.320
or you can send it
via MQQT to IoT Core.

00:44:27.320 --> 00:44:29.782
So it should be able to support
other platforms as well.

00:44:40.451 --> 00:44:40.950
OK.

00:44:40.950 --> 00:44:41.830
Thanks for coming.

00:44:41.830 --> 00:44:44.288
And if you have more questions,
I will be standing outside.

00:44:44.288 --> 00:44:45.270
Thank you.

